Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "BigSur version 2.0 (",
 StyleBox["BigSur2",
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0]],
 ")"
}], "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.998676883911522*^9, 
  3.998676914903647*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3dc8f65c-75db-4865-9e77-\
8b17a5c82c28"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Version 09172026\n\nChanges since version 1.0:\n\t1.  The value of the \
underlying coefficient of variation of gene expression noise is no longer \
assumed to be independent of the magnitude of gene expression, but to follow \
a formula.  The key parameter in that formula is \[Theta] (see FindTheta, \
above)\n\t2.  The moment calculations used to obtain p-values for Fano \
factors and correlation coefficients are now based on a Negative Binomial \
Distribution (parametrized by the observed mean and calculated coefficient of \
variation) rather than a Poisson LogNormal Distribution.  This change \
produces very little difference in output, but better aligns with theory of \
bursty gene expression\n\t3.  Changes have been made to some algorithms to \
speed up calculation times, especially when cell numbers are large.  \n\n\
Arguments are:  \n",
 StyleBox["matpac",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ":  See \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]what is a matpac\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
above.  Before proceeding with Big Sur one should remove any genes with zeros \
for all entries.  In addition, one should make sure that the list of gene \
names and list of cell IDs do not contain any duplicates.  \n",
 StyleBox["noiserules",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": a set of rules for substituting \[Eta] and \[Theta].  It should be of the \
form {\[Eta]->..., \[Theta]-> ....}, and would normally be the output of \
FindTheta (see above). \n",
 StyleBox["filemarker",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ":  text indicating the beginning of the names of files that will be \
generated and saved.  \n",
 StyleBox["depthscaling",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ":  See \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]FindBestCAlt\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] above.  \
If anything else is put in (e.g. \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Auto\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]) for this argument the code will calculate the \
depthscaling itself, from the data.  \n\nOutputs:  \nThe outputs are a series \
of files, containing information like the modified corrected residual matrix, \
the modified corrected fano factors and their -log10 transformed pvalues, \
modified corrected PCCs, pvalues of significant gene gene correlations, and a \
gNO containing the equivalent PCC values of the significant correlations.  \n\
\nExplanation of options:\n\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Depthscaling\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]:  Default ={}.  Under default conditions, the list \
of sequencing depths for all the cells is calculated direct from the data.  \
The alternative is that one can substitute an explicit list of numbers, in \
which case it must have the same length as the number of cells in the matpac. \
 One might do that if one is performing BigSur on only a subset of genes in a \
dataset, bu",
 StyleBox["t wants to use all the genes to calculate the relative sequencing \
depths of the cells (which would presumably be a little more accurate).  In \
that case, one could use the formula ",
  Background->None],
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"Total", "/@", 
     RowBox[{"N", "[", 
      RowBox[{"Transpose", "[", "mat", "]"}], "]"}]}], 
    RowBox[{"Total", "[", 
     RowBox[{"Flatten", "[", "mat", "]"}], "]"}]],
   Background->None]],
